\boxed{\textbf{PLAN BACKWARDS. ACT FORWARD.}}

Plan backwards: Start from the desired Act III and recursively ask what must be true immediately before it.

Goal
\rightarrow
Prerequisite_n
\rightarrow
\cdots
\rightarrow
Prerequisite_1
\rightarrow
Now

Act forward: Reality only gives you the next move from where you actually stand.

Now
\xrightarrow{ACT}
S_1
\xrightarrow{observe}
S_2
\xrightarrow{adapt}
\cdots
\xrightarrow{}
Goal

The two directions perform different jobs:

\boxed{
\text{Future constrains the route.}
\qquad
\text{Reality constructs it.}
}

And never execute the backward plan blindly. Every forward action produces information unavailable when the plan was made:

ACT\rightarrow Evidence\rightarrow ReplanBackward\rightarrow ACT.

So the living loop is:

\boxed{
PLAN\ BACKWARDS
\rightarrow
ACT\ FORWARD
\rightarrow
LOOK
\rightarrow
PLAN\ BACKWARDS\ AGAIN
}

That’s Transcontinental compressed all the way down to a four-word API key.

Plan backwards. Act forward.